\begin{abstract}
We give a complete, self-contained and resource-abstract account of three
successive limits on data compression, and prove that the third is exactly the
cryptographic hardness boundary. First, an unconditional counting theorem: for
an arbitrary decompressor $D$ --- any map from bit strings to objects --- at most
$2^{s+1}-1$ objects admit a $D$-program of length at most $s$; consequently some
string of every length $s+1$ is incompressible, and at most a $2^{-(c-1)}$
fraction of length-$n$ strings compress to $n-c$ bits. Second, a
\emph{seed-budget theorem}: a seeded family of decompressors indexed by a finite
seed space $R$ compresses at most $|R|(2^{s+1}-1)$ objects to $s$ bits, and this
is tight, since with all $2^{k}$ seeds of length $k$ the ``seed carries the
prefix'' family compresses every string of length $k+s$ to $s$ bits; conversely a
single deterministic decompressor simulates any seeded family at additive cost
$2k+1$. Randomness therefore helps worst-case compression by exactly the seed
length and no more. Third, an equivalence between compression and cryptography:
relative to any class of algorithms closed under \emph{length guarding} and
\emph{bounded search over the guard parameter}, inverting all honest functions of
the class is equivalent to solving the shortest-program problem for all honest
decompressors of the class; hence one-way functions exist for the class if and
only if compression search is hard for it. The equivalence survives arbitrary
additive approximation slack, and a decision oracle for the prefix-compressibility
predicate already yields an optimal-program finder by bit-by-bit reconstruction.
Finally, the resulting description gap is not a finite artefact: for any
collection of algorithms closed under finite patching in which $f$ is
uninvertible, every algorithm of the collection fails on an infinite set of
inputs; an explicit witnessing collection and function are exhibited.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

Fix a way of interpreting descriptions as objects. How short can a description
be, and can it be found? The first half of the question has a classical answer:
counting. The second half --- whether the short description can be \emph{produced
by an algorithm} --- is where the subject becomes computational and, as we show,
cryptographic.

Statements of the form ``shortest-program finding is as hard as inverting one-way
functions'' are folklore, but their proofs are usually entangled with a machine
model, a time bound, and an error probability. The reduction actually uses only
two closure properties of the class of algorithms in question: the ability to
form a \emph{length-guarded} variant of a function, and the ability to run a
\emph{bounded linear search} over the guard parameter while calling a family of
subroutines. We axiomatize exactly those two properties; every resource
discipline satisfying them is covered at once, and the hypotheses genuinely
needed become visible.

\paragraph{Results.}
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Theorem A} (Pigeonhole Ceiling): at most $2^{s+1}-1$ objects have $D$-complexity $\le s$, for any $D$.
  \item \textbf{Theorem B} (Seed-Budget): a seeded family with seed space $R$ compresses at most $|R|(2^{s+1}-1)$ objects to $s$ bits.
  \item \textbf{Theorem C}: the bound of Theorem B is achieved by the prefix family, and derandomization costs $2k+1$ additive bits.
  \item \textbf{Theorem D} (Main Equivalence): for a search-closed class, inverting all honest functions is equivalent to optimally compressing for all honest decompressors.
  \item \textbf{Theorem E}: one-way functions exist for the class iff compression search is hard for it; likewise for approximate compression search with arbitrary slack.
  \item \textbf{Theorem F} (Search to Decision): a prefix-compressibility oracle yields an optimal-program finder, hence an inverter.
  \item \textbf{Theorems G, H}: under one-wayness every algorithm misses a string with a short description --- infinitely many, once the collection is closed under finite patching.
\end{itemize}

\section{Description systems and complexity}

Throughout, $\Str$ is the set of finite bit strings, $|p|$ the length of $p$,
$\cat$ concatenation, $\varepsilon$ the empty string, and $\alpha$ an arbitrary
set of objects.

\begin{definition}
A \emph{decompressor} is any function $D : \Str \to \alpha$. An object $y$ is
\emph{describable} under $D$ if $D(p)=y$ for some $p$, called a \emph{$D$-program}
for $y$. The \emph{complexity} of $y$ is
\[ K_D(y) \;=\; \inf\{\, n \in \mathbb{N} : \exists p,\ |p|=n \wedge D(p)=y \,\}, \]
with $K_D(y)=0$ when $y$ is not describable.
\end{definition}

No computability requirement is placed on $D$: the counting theorems must hold
against arbitrary adversarial decompressors, while the later sections restrict to
a class of algorithms and ask what such a class can \emph{find}.

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:basic}
(i) $D(p)=y$ implies $K_D(y)\le|p|$. (ii) If $y$ is describable there is $p$ with
$|p|=K_D(y)$ and $D(p)=y$. (iii) $(\exists p,\ |p|\le s \wedge D(p)=y)$ iff
($y$ describable and $K_D(y)\le s$).
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
(i) is immediate. For (ii), the set of program lengths for $y$ is a nonempty set
of naturals, hence attains its infimum by well-ordering. (iii) follows from (i)
and (ii).
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}
Define $\nu:\Str\to\mathbb{N}$ by $\nu(\varepsilon)=1$ and
$\nu(b\cat t)=2\nu(t)+[b]$, where $[b]\in\{0,1\}$ is the value of the bit $b$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:numeral}
$\nu$ is injective, $\nu(p)\ge1$, and $\nu(p)<2^{|p|+1}$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Positivity and the bound are inductions: $\nu(b\cat t)=2\nu(t)+[b]<2\cdot2^{|t|+1}$.
Injectivity is an induction on the first argument: positivity separates
$\varepsilon$ from a nonempty string, and parity recovers the leading bit in the
remaining case.
\end{proof}

Thus $p\mapsto\nu(p)-1$ injects the strings of length $\le s$ into
$\{0,\dots,2^{s+1}-2\}$, a set of size $2^{s+1}-1$.

\section{The unconditional ceiling}

\begin{theorem}[Pigeonhole Ceiling]\label{thm:ceiling}
Let $D:\Str\to\alpha$ be any decompressor, $s\in\mathbb{N}$, and $T$ a finite set
of objects each describable with $K_D(y)\le s$. Then $|T|\le 2^{s+1}-1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By Lemma~\ref{lem:basic}(iii) choose for each $y\in T$ a program $\pi(y)$ with
$|\pi(y)|\le s$ and $D(\pi(y))=y$. The map $y\mapsto\nu(\pi(y))-1$ sends $T$ into
$\{0,\dots,2^{s+1}-2\}$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:numeral}, and is injective on $T$: equal
values force $\pi(y)=\pi(z)$ and hence $y=D(\pi(y))=D(\pi(z))=z$. Compare
cardinalities.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Incompressible strings exist]\label{thm:incompressible}
For every $D:\Str\to\Str$ and every $s$, some string of length $s+1$ is not
compressible to $s$ bits.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Otherwise the $2^{s+1}$ strings of length $s+1$ satisfy the hypothesis of
Theorem~\ref{thm:ceiling}, giving $2^{s+1}\le2^{s+1}-1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Density of incompressibility]
For $D:\Str\to\Str$ and $1\le c\le n$, if $m$ is the number of strings of length
$n$ compressible to $n-c$ bits, then $2^{c-1}m \le 2^{n}$; that is, at most a
$2^{-(c-1)}$ fraction of length-$n$ strings can be compressed by $c$ bits.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Theorem~\ref{thm:ceiling} gives $m\le 2^{n-c+1}-1$; multiply by $2^{c-1}$ and use
$2^{c-1}2^{n-c+1}=2^{n}$.
\end{proof}

\section{Randomness: the seed budget and its tightness}

\begin{definition}
A \emph{seeded decompression system} is a family $D:R\to(\Str\to\alpha)$ with $R$
finite. An object $y$ is \emph{$s$-compressible} under it if some seed $r$ has $y$
describable under $D_r$ with $K_{D_r}(y)\le s$. The seed is not charged to the
program length.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Seed-Budget Theorem]\label{thm:seed}
If every element of a finite set $T$ is $s$-compressible under a seeded system
with seed space $R$, then $|T|\le |R|\,(2^{s+1}-1)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Choose for each $y\in T$ a pair $(r_y,p_y)$ with $|p_y|\le s$ and $D_{r_y}(p_y)=y$.
Then $y\mapsto(r_y,\nu(p_y)-1)$ injects $T$ into $R\times\{0,\dots,2^{s+1}-2\}$ by
the fibrewise version of the argument of Theorem~\ref{thm:ceiling}.
\end{proof}

With $|R|=2^{k}$: $k$ random bits buy at most $k+1$ bits of compression.

\begin{definition}
For $r\in\Str$ let $P_r(p)=r\cat p$ (the \emph{prefix family}).
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Matching construction]\label{thm:prefix}
For all $k,s$ and every $y$ with $|y|=k+s$ there is a seed $r$ of length $k$ with
$y$ describable under $P_r$ and $K_{P_r}(y)\le s$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Let $r$ be the first $k$ bits of $y$ and $p$ the remaining $s$ bits.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Randomness gain is exactly the seed length]
For all $k,s$: (1) all $2^{k}2^{s}$ strings of length $k+s$ are compressed to $s$
bits by the prefix family with the $2^{k}$ seeds of length $k$; and (2) no seeded
family with seed space $R$ compresses more than $|R|(2^{s+1}-1)$ objects to $s$
bits.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
(1) is Theorem~\ref{thm:prefix} with the count $2^{k+s}$; (2) is
Theorem~\ref{thm:seed}.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Derandomization at the seed price}

\begin{definition}\label{def:pairing}
For $i\in\mathbb{N}$ and $p\in\Str$ let $\mathrm{tag}(i,p)=\one^{i}\cat\zero\cat p$,
so $|\mathrm{tag}(i,p)|=i+1+|p|$ and the pair is recoverable by reading the leading
run of ones. For strings let $\langle p,q\rangle=\mathrm{tag}(|p|,p\cat q)$, so
$|\langle p,q\rangle| = 2|p|+1+|q|$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}
For a family $D$ indexed by strings, let $(\mathrm{Idx}\,D)(z)=D_r(p)$ where
$(r,p)$ is the decoding of $z$ under $\langle\cdot,\cdot\rangle$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Derandomization cost]\label{thm:derand}
For every seeded family $D$, seed $r$, and $y$ describable under $D_r$,
\[ K_{\mathrm{Idx}\,D}(y) \;\le\; 2|r|+1+K_{D_r}(y). \]
In particular $|r|=k$ and $K_{D_r}(y)\le s$ give $K_{\mathrm{Idx}\,D}(y)\le 2k+1+s$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Feed $\langle r,p\rangle$ for a shortest $D_r$-program $p$, and apply the length
identity of Definition~\ref{def:pairing}.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Universality and subadditivity}

\begin{definition}
For a family $D:\mathbb{N}\to(\Str\to\Str)$ let $U_D(z)=D_i(p)$ where
$(i,p)=\mathrm{tag}^{-1}(z)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Invariance]\label{thm:invariance}
If $y$ is describable under $D_i$ then $K_{U_D}(y)\le K_{D_i}(y)+i+1$; moreover
$y$ is describable under $U_D$ iff it is describable under some $D_i$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Subadditivity]
With $(D_1\otimes D_2)(z)=D_1(p)\cat D_2(q)$ for $(p,q)$ the decoding of $z$, and
$x,y$ describable,
$K_{D_1\otimes D_2}(x\cat y)\le 2K_{D_1}(x)+1+K_{D_2}(y)$.
\end{theorem}

\section{Compression search and inversion}

\begin{definition}
$A$ \emph{inverts} $f:\Str\to\Str$ if $f(A(y))=y$ for every $y$ in the range of $f$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}
$A$ is a \emph{shortest-program finder} for $D$ if for every describable $y$,
$D(A(y))=y$ and $|A(y)|=K_D(y)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:dominates}
Every shortest-program finder for $D$ inverts $D$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{definition}[Length guard]
For $f:\Str\to\Str$ and $l\in\mathbb{N}$,
\[ f_l(p) \;=\; \begin{cases} \one\cat f(p), & |p|\le l,\\ \zero\cat p, & |p|>l.\end{cases}\]
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Guard semantics]\label{lem:guard}
$\one\cat y$ is describable under $f_l$ iff $y$ is describable under $f$ with
$K_f(y)\le l$. If $A_l$ inverts $f_l$ and $K_f(y)\le l$, then
$A_l(\one\cat y)$ is an $f$-program for $y$ of length at most $l$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{definition}[Bounded search]
$\leastop(P,F)$ is the least $l\le F$ with $P(l)$, computed by linear scan. If
some $l\le F$ satisfies $P$, then $P(\leastop(P,F))$ holds and $P(m)$ fails for
all $m<\leastop(P,F)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Assembled compressor]\label{def:sf}
\[ \mathrm{SF}_{f,A,F}(y) = A_{l^\star}(\one\cat y),\qquad
   l^\star = \leastop\bigl(l\mapsto [\,f_l(A_l(\one\cat y))=\one\cat y\,],\,F(|y|)\bigr). \]
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Inversion solves compression search]\label{thm:sfcorrect}
Suppose $A_l$ inverts $f_l$ for every $l$. Then for every describable $y$ with
$K_f(y)\le F(|y|)$,
$f(\mathrm{SF}_{f,A,F}(y))=y$ and $|\mathrm{SF}_{f,A,F}(y)|=K_f(y)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Let $P$ be the tested predicate. Then $P(l)\iff K_f(y)\le l$: if $K_f(y)\le l$
then $\one\cat y$ lies in the range of $f_l$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:guard} and the
inverter succeeds; conversely $P(l)$ forces $|A_l(\one\cat y)|\le l$ and
$f(A_l(\one\cat y))=y$, so $K_f(y)\le l$. Hence the search returns
$l^\star=K_f(y)$, and the output is a program of length $\le l^\star$, hence
exactly $K_f(y)$ by minimality.
\end{proof}

\section{Classes of algorithms and the equivalence}

\begin{definition}[Search-closed class]\label{def:class}
A \emph{search-closed class} $\mathcal{C}$ consists of a set
$\Comp(\mathcal{C})\subseteq\{\Str\to\Str\}$ and a predicate $\Allowed(\mathcal{C})$
on bounds $b:\mathbb{N}\to\mathbb{N}$ such that
(F1) all constant bounds are allowed;
(F2) $n\mapsto n$ is allowed;
(F3) allowed bounds are closed under pointwise maximum;
(G) $f\in\Comp(\mathcal{C})$ implies $f_l\in\Comp(\mathcal{C})$ for all $l$;
(S) if $f\in\Comp(\mathcal{C})$, $A_l\in\Comp(\mathcal{C})$ for all $l$ and $b$ is
allowed, then $\mathrm{SF}_{f,A,b}\in\Comp(\mathcal{C})$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Honesty]
$f$ is \emph{honest in $\mathcal{C}$} if some allowed $b$ satisfies
$K_f(y)\le b(|y|)$ for every describable $y$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Guards preserve honesty]\label{lem:honestguard}
Under (F1)--(F3), $f_l$ is honest in $\mathcal{C}$ with bound $n\mapsto\max(l,n)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
If $f_l(p)=y$ with $|p|\le l$ then $K_{f_l}(y)\le l$; otherwise $y=\zero\cat p$
so $|y|=|p|+1$ and $K_{f_l}(y)\le|p|\le|y|$.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}
$f$ is \emph{one-way in $\mathcal{C}$} if $f\in\Comp(\mathcal{C})$, $f$ is honest,
and no $A\in\Comp(\mathcal{C})$ inverts $f$. The compression-search problem for
$D$ is \emph{hard for $\mathcal{C}$} if $D\in\Comp(\mathcal{C})$, $D$ is honest,
and no $A\in\Comp(\mathcal{C})$ is a shortest-program finder for $D$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Main equivalence]\label{thm:main}
For every search-closed class $\mathcal{C}$ the following are equivalent:
(1) every honest $f\in\Comp(\mathcal{C})$ is inverted by some $A\in\Comp(\mathcal{C})$;
(2) every honest $D\in\Comp(\mathcal{C})$ admits a shortest-program finder in
$\Comp(\mathcal{C})$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
(2)$\Rightarrow$(1) is Proposition~\ref{prop:dominates}. For (1)$\Rightarrow$(2),
let $f$ be honest with allowed bound $b$. By (G) each $f_l$ is in the class and by
Lemma~\ref{lem:honestguard} honest, so (1) supplies inverters $A_l$ in the class;
(S) puts $\mathrm{SF}_{f,A,b}$ in the class, and Theorem~\ref{thm:sfcorrect} --- whose
fuel hypothesis is exactly honesty --- makes it a shortest-program finder.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[One-way functions iff hardness of compression search]\label{thm:owf}
For every search-closed class $\mathcal{C}$, a one-way function exists in
$\mathcal{C}$ if and only if some decompressor has compression search hard for
$\mathcal{C}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
($\Rightarrow$) A one-way $f$ is an honest member of the class and a
shortest-program finder for it would invert it. ($\Leftarrow$) If no one-way
function exists, every honest member is invertible in the class, so by
Theorem~\ref{thm:main} every honest member admits a finder, contradicting hardness.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Robustness I: approximation}

\begin{definition}
$A$ is a \emph{$\delta$-approximate} shortest-program finder for $D$ if for every
describable $y$: $D(A(y))=y$ and $|A(y)|\le K_D(y)+\delta(|y|)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Approximation does not help]
For every search-closed class and \emph{every} slack $\delta$, statement (1) of
Theorem~\ref{thm:main} is equivalent to: every honest $D$ of the class admits a
$\delta$-approximate shortest-program finder in the class. Hence one-way functions
exist iff approximate compression search is hard.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
An exact finder is $\delta$-approximate; an approximate finder still satisfies
$D(A(y))=y$ and hence inverts $D$. Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:main}.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Robustness II: decision versus search}

\begin{definition}
$\dect(y,w,n)$ is a \emph{correct prefix oracle} for $D$ if
$\dect(y,w,n)=\text{true}\iff\exists p,\ |p|=n\wedge D(w\cat p)=y$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}
$\rebuild(0,w)=w$ and
\[ \rebuild(n+1,w)=\begin{cases}\rebuild(n,\,w\cat\zero) & \text{if }\dect(y,w\cat\zero,n),\\
\rebuild(n,\,w\cat\one) & \text{otherwise.}\end{cases}\]
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Correctness of reconstruction]\label{lem:rebuild}
If $\dect$ is a correct prefix oracle for $D$ and some length-$n$ continuation of
$w$ is a $D$-program for $y$, then $D(\rebuild(n,w))=y$ and
$|\rebuild(n,w)|=|w|+n$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Induction on $n$. For $n=0$ the continuation is empty. For $n+1$, write a
surviving continuation as $b\cat t$ with $|t|=n$: if the oracle accepts
$w\cat\zero$, apply the induction hypothesis there; otherwise the $\zero$-branch is
dead, so $b=\one$ and the induction hypothesis applies to $w\cat\one$ with $t$.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}
$\mathrm{DF}_{\dect,F}(y)=\rebuild\bigl(\leastop(n\mapsto\dect(y,\varepsilon,n),F(|y|)),\ \varepsilon\bigr)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Search to decision]\label{thm:searchdec}
If $\dect$ is a correct prefix oracle for $D$ and $K_D(y)\le F(|y|)$ for every
describable $y$, then $\mathrm{DF}_{\dect,F}$ is a shortest-program finder for $D$,
and in particular inverts $D$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
At the empty prefix the oracle says whether a program of length exactly $n$
exists, so the search returns $n^\star=K_D(y)$; Lemma~\ref{lem:rebuild} then
delivers a program of that exact length.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[No prefix oracle under one-wayness]
If $f$ is one-way in $\mathcal{C}$ and $\dect$ is a correct prefix oracle for $f$
with allowed fuel $F$ covering $K_f$, then $\mathrm{DF}_{\dect,F}\notin\Comp(\mathcal{C})$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{corollary}
For any decompressor $D$, an exact shortest-program finder, a $\delta$-approximate
finder, and a correct prefix oracle each yield an inverter for $D$; and inside a
search-closed class, invertibility of all honest members yields all three back.
The tasks sit at the same cryptographic level.
\end{corollary}

\begin{remark}
The reduction of Theorem~\ref{thm:searchdec} consumes \emph{conditional}
information: it asks about continuations of a prefix. An unconditional oracle
returning only the number $K_D(y)$ is not known to suffice without an additional
closure assumption; see Conjecture~\ref{conj:est}.
\end{remark}

\section{Consequences for achievable worst-case bounds}

\begin{theorem}[Description gap]\label{thm:gap}
Let $f$ be one-way in $\mathcal{C}$. There is an allowed bound $b$ such that for
every $A\in\Comp(\mathcal{C})$ there is a string $y$ describable under $f$ with
$K_f(y)\le b(|y|)$ and $f(A(y))\neq y$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Honesty supplies $b$; negating the inversion condition for $A$ supplies $y$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Universality does not close the gap]
If $f$ is one-way in $\mathcal{C}$ and $f=D_i$ for a family $D$, then there is an
allowed $b$ such that every $A\in\Comp(\mathcal{C})$ misses some $y$ with
$K_{U_D}(y)\le b(|y|)+i+1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Combine Theorem~\ref{thm:gap} with the invariance Theorem~\ref{thm:invariance}.
\end{proof}

\subsection{The failures are infinite}

\begin{definition}
A set $\mathcal{A}$ of algorithms is \emph{patch-closed} if for every
$A\in\mathcal{A}$, every finite $F\subseteq\Str$ and every $g$, the map
$y\mapsto g(y)$ for $y\in F$ and $y\mapsto A(y)$ otherwise lies in $\mathcal{A}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Infinite failure of inversion]\label{thm:inf}
Let $\mathcal{A}$ be patch-closed and suppose no $A\in\mathcal{A}$ inverts $f$.
Then for every $A\in\mathcal{A}$ the set
$\{y : y \text{ describable under } f \wedge f(A(y))\neq y\}$ is infinite.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
If the failure set were a finite set $F$, choose for each describable $y$ some
preimage $g(y)$ and patch $A$ with $g$ on $F$. The patched algorithm lies in
$\mathcal{A}$ and inverts $f$ everywhere, a contradiction.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Infinite failure of compression]
Under the same hypotheses, for every $A\in\mathcal{A}$ the set of describable $y$
for which $A(y)$ is not a shortest $f$-program for $y$ is infinite.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
It contains the set of Theorem~\ref{thm:inf}.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[The hypotheses are satisfiable]
Let $\mathcal{T}$ be the set of algorithms agreeing with $y\mapsto\mathrm{tail}(y)$
on only finitely many inputs, and $\tau(p)=\one\cat p$. Then $\mathcal{T}$ is
patch-closed, $\tau\in\mathcal{T}$, and no $A\in\mathcal{T}$ inverts $\tau$; hence
every $A\in\mathcal{T}$ fails to invert $\tau$ on infinitely many inputs.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The agreement set of a patched algorithm is contained in the union of the original
agreement set and the finite patch domain. $\tau(y)$ and $\mathrm{tail}(y)$ have
different lengths, so $\tau$'s agreement set is empty. An inverter for $\tau$ must
map $\one\cat p$ to $p$ for every $p$, making its agreement set infinite.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}\label{rem:tension}
Patch closure and axiom (S) pull in opposite directions: (S) forces uniform
control of output lengths in the guard parameter, whereas finite patching destroys
it. Whether a single class satisfies both and contains a one-way function is open.
\end{remark}

\subsection{Non-vacuity of the class axioms}

The class of \emph{all} functions with all bounds allowed satisfies
Definition~\ref{def:class}; it has no one-way function and, consistently with
Theorem~\ref{thm:owf}, compression search is easy for it. The \emph{length class}
$\{g : |p|\le|g(p)| \text{ for all } p\}$, with all bounds allowed, is also
search-closed, and the tagging function $\tau(p)=\one\cat p$ is one-way in it,
since an inverter would have to shorten its input. Hence compression search is
hard for the length class.

\section{The calibration theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Calibration]
Let $\mathcal{C}$ be a search-closed class admitting a one-way function and let
$s,k\in\mathbb{N}$. Then simultaneously:
(1) for every decompressor $D$, at most $2^{s+1}-1$ objects have complexity $\le s$;
(2) for every decompressor some string of length $s+1$ resists compression to $s$ bits;
(3) every string of length $k+s$ is compressible to $s$ bits by a seeded family with
$2^{k}$ seeds of length $k$, while no seeded family with seed space $R$ beats
$|R|(2^{s+1}-1)$, and derandomizing costs $2k+1$ additive bits;
(4) there is a decompressor of the class for which no algorithm of the class ever
outputs shortest programs, so there are strings with short descriptions that no
algorithm of the class produces --- infinitely many per algorithm when the
collection is patch-closed.
\end{theorem}

In words: randomness helps compression exactly up to the seed length and no
further; efficient compression then stops again at the cryptographic hardness
boundary, whether one demands exact optimality, approximate optimality within any
slack, or a mere yes/no decision.

\section{Discussion and open problems}

Two attempted strengthenings failed instructively. An \emph{unconditional} length
oracle, returning $K_D(y)$ with no structural information, could not be converted
into an inverter without extra closure; the reduction genuinely uses the
conditional prefix predicate. And honesty is not cosmetic: without it the bounded
search may exhaust its fuel before reaching $K_f(y)$, and a function can be
one-way merely because its preimages are astronomically long.

\begin{conjecture}[Unconditional estimation gap]\label{conj:est}
There is a search-closed class $\mathcal{C}$ and $D\in\Comp(\mathcal{C})$ such that
the unconditional length-estimation task (output the number $K_D(y)$) is solvable
in $\mathcal{C}$ while no algorithm of $\mathcal{C}$ is a shortest-program finder
for $D$. A separating class should be closed under guarding and bounded search but
\emph{not} under prefix restriction $D\mapsto(p\mapsto D(w\cat p))$.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[Honesty is necessary]
There is a search-closed class $\mathcal{C}$ and a non-honest $f\in\Comp(\mathcal{C})$
that is not invertible in $\mathcal{C}$, while every honest decompressor of
$\mathcal{C}$ admits a shortest-program finder in $\mathcal{C}$.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[Quantitative gap theorem]
For every search-closed class with a one-way $f$ and every algorithm $A$ of the
class the failure set is infinite; moreover beyond some threshold every length
interval contains a failure. The infinitude is established here under patch
closure; the uniform per-length version, and the reconciliation noted in
Remark~\ref{rem:tension}, remain open.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[Compatibility]
Some collection of algorithms is simultaneously search-closed and patch-closed and
contains a one-way function.
\end{conjecture}

Further directions include an average-case calibration over samplable
distributions; a model in which seeds are charged fractionally, interpolating
between the seed-budget theorem and the derandomization bound and connecting to
pseudorandom generators; and a multi-bit guard encoding the length itself, which
would give a search-free reduction at the price of a stronger closure axiom.

\paragraph{Interpretation.} Randomness buys exactly its own length in bits: the
seed is a description moved off the ledger, and derandomization moves it back on
at almost no loss. Beyond that, the only remaining obstacle to compressing an
object that \emph{has} a short description is the difficulty of searching for that
description --- and that difficulty is precisely the assumption on which modern
cryptography rests.

